    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe duplicate-detection logic in `POST /api/owners` previously ran three independent 409 checks (telephone, email, household). I consolidated them into a single derived `identityKey`.\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a derived `@Transient getIdentityKey()`:\n   ```\n   normalizedTelephone + \"|\" + (email or empty) + \"|\" + (householdId or empty)\n   ```\n   The stored telephone is already E.164-normalized and the email already lower-cased, so the key is built from the canonical stored forms.\n\n2. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `identityKey` string to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and it's **returned** in create/get responses (MapStruct maps it automatically from the new getter).\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 replaced the three `rejectDuplicate*` calls with a single `rejectDuplicateIdentity(owner)`, invoked after telephone/email/household are set. The old `sharesHousehold == false \u2192 rejectDuplicateHousehold` branch is gone; `assignHousehold` is still called for the opt-in case so the household id feeds the key. The new check rejects with 409 only when the **whole** key matches an existing owner's.\n\n4. **Exceptions/advice** \u2014 added `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException` + its 409 handler (reporting `errors: [\"identityKey\"]`); deleted the now-obsolete `DuplicateOwnerTelephone/Email/Household` exception classes and their handlers/constants.\n\n## Behaviour change (as specified)\n- Two members of the same household (same `householdId`) with **different** telephones now get different identity keys and are both allowed \u2014 only an exact full-key match is a duplicate.\n- The acceptance test `coreRejectsIdentityCollision` (two otherwise-unique owners sharing a telephone \u2192 both keys `\"+61\u2026||\"`) correctly returns 409.\n\nNo commit was made, since you didn't ask for one.",
